    "turn_idx": 76,
    "turn_text": "Beach, California. California, okay. Perfect example there. Obviously, California is a microcosm of what you just talked about, the disassociation, the angst, the division, people not pulling together. That was a great song, by the way. We are the world. It zoomed to the top of the charts around the nation because it was so meaningful. I remember when that song came out and I remember we were in a contentious time. There was a lot of divisiveness. People were looking around and began to distrust people and of course here we are. We're right back in the same place but it's at a much different level. I'm 67 years old and I can say from my perspective I've been through Vietnam. I didn't go there, but I was here. I remember Kent State, all of the places where people were out demonstrating there were protests, there were riots, and several Kent State students were actually shot and killed by National Guardsmen doing that. I remember how tough it was then, but I think we're at a level now that we never I've never seen in my lifetime and we hear stories about it. But do you think is there really a way using the Olympics or some other thing? Is there any way that we can find consensus of some sort with which we could really affect some kind of healing of our nation? Do you think we can do even do that now?",
